./worklog/worklog_yixun/exp_11_fa_orbit_claude/fa_orbit_codex_chunk_kit_review.md:5061:   Treating `CANCELLED` as non-success is correct, but automatically replacing a human-cancelled job is not. `CANCELLED` should produce an immediate hold/freeze or require an explicit pause/resume action. Currently the first cancellation is resubmitted, and its failure can even be cleared if an endpoint checkpoint exists.
./worklog/worklog_yixun/exp_11_fa_orbit_claude/fa_orbit_codex_chunk_kit_review.md:5065:5. Chunk checkpoints are incompatible with the existing evaluation lineage gate. The chunk recorder writes `arms.<ARM>.chain`, but `fa_orbit_screen.sbatch` still calls `fa_orbit_producer_manifest.verify_chain`, which only accepts entries under `restarts.<ARM>` with producer manifests. Consequently every chunk-produced checkpoint above 40k is rejected. See [fa_orbit_screen.sbatch](/n/fs/gatrdp/codespace/FLAC/worklog/worklog_yixun/exp_11_fa_orbit_claude/fa_orbit_screen.sbatch:489) and [fa_orbit_producer_manifest.py](/n/fs/gatrdp/codespace/FLAC/worklog/worklog_yixun/exp_11_fa_orbit_claude/fa_orbit_producer_manifest.py:223).
./worklog/worklog_yixun/exp_11_fa_orbit_claude/fa_orbit_codex_chunk_kit_review.md:5067:6. The lineage can still attribute a stale/foreign compatible checkpoint to the wrong job. A launcher manifest is published before training, and the recorder associates it with whichever exactly-named endpoint is present in the shared directory. It does not require `sacct=COMPLETED`, the classifier completion record, or a job-generated endpoint attestation. Thus a failed job’s manifest plus a pre-existing same-arm/config checkpoint can become a link; rehashing proves current bytes, not which job produced them.
./worklog/worklog_yixun/exp_11_fa_orbit_claude/fa_orbit_codex_chunk_kit_review.md:5071:7. VANL is not launch-ready. Its live registry row lacks both `final_ckpt_sha256` and `final_step`, while the default watchdog includes VANL. Its first chunk will be submitted and then rejected by `--extension`. The 40k checkpoint must be audited and anchored before startup, and the watchdog should validate all selected anchors before submitting anything. See [arm_launch_registry.json](/n/fs/gatrdp/codespace/FLAC/worklog/worklog_yixun/exp_11_fa_orbit_claude/arm_launch_registry.json:110).
./worklog/worklog_yixun/exp_11_fa_orbit_claude/fa_orbit_codex_chunk_kit_review.md:5079:- At 2,500 steps, 10–15 minutes of startup adds roughly 20–28% of total leg time for VANL and 14–19% for C4L; VANL is unacceptably overhead-heavy and C4L is borderline. A 5,000-step VANL chunk fits its 2.5-hour pin comfortably; C4L would be close to its 3-hour pin and deserves slightly more margin. C16/C32 should remain at 2,500. The current global `CHUNK` option should not permit 5,000 universally: C32 cannot fit 5,000 steps in its 9-hour pin.
